# Scannable reference sections for the guide column. Imported by guides.py.
GUIDE = {}

def _r(slug, html):
    GUIDE[slug] = html

_r('tax-calculator', '''<div class="g-ref"><h2>2026 wage-tax reference</h2>
<p>This estimate is for wages in tax year 2026. Federal ordinary brackets and the standard deduction follow IRS Revenue Procedure 2025-32. Employee Social Security is 6.2 percent up to the Social Security Administration wage base of $184,500. Medicare is 1.45 percent of all wages, plus 0.9 percent Additional Medicare Tax above $200,000 single or head of household, $250,000 married filing jointly, and $125,000 married filing separately. The 401(k) elective deferral modeled here stops at $24,500. State tables follow the Tax Foundation's 2026 individual income-tax compilation: standard deduction, brackets, and a personal exemption or credit where that table records one. Sources: the <a href="https://www.irs.gov/newsroom/irs-releases-tax-inflation-adjustments-for-tax-year-2026-including-amendments-from-the-one-big-beautiful-bill">IRS 2026 inflation adjustments</a>, the <a href="https://www.ssa.gov/news/press/factsheets/colafacts2026.pdf">SSA 2026 COLA fact sheet</a>, and the <a href="https://taxfoundation.org/data/all/state/state-income-tax-rates-2026/">Tax Foundation 2026 state brackets</a>.</p>
<div class="g-ex"><h3>Worked example: $80,000, single, Texas</h3>
<p>Taxable income is 80,000 minus the $16,100 standard deduction, or $63,900. Federal tax is 10 percent of $12,400 ($1,240), plus 12 percent of the next $38,000 ($4,560), plus 22 percent of the remaining $13,500 ($2,970), which totals $8,770. Social Security is $4,960. Medicare is $1,160. Total tax is $14,890 and take-home pay is $65,110. The marginal federal bracket is 22 percent. The effective rate on gross pay is 18.61 percent. A $5,000 pre-tax 401(k) saves 22 percent, or $1,100, of federal income tax and does not reduce FICA. Cash in hand becomes $61,210.</p></div>
<h3>Same wages in Georgia</h3>
<p>Georgia's simplified 2026 row is 5.19 percent after a $12,000 single standard deduction. State tax is (80,000 minus 12,000) times 0.0519, or $3,529.20. Federal and FICA do not change, so take-home pay falls to about $61,581. Nine states in this model have no wage income tax. Washington's capital-gains tax is not a wage tax, so wages show zero state income tax. City taxes and California SDI are not included.</p>
<h3>What to do with the number</h3>
<p>Match the lines to a pay stub before you change a W-4. Sole proprietors should not paste profit into the salary field: self-employment tax is 15.3 percent of 92.35 percent of net earnings and is a different calculation. Use the <a href="../paycheck-calculator/">paycheck calculator</a> for hourly and overtime, and the <a href="../budget-calculator/">budget calculator</a> once you know monthly take-home. Exports are labeled estimates, not a return.</p></div>''')

_r('paycheck-calculator', '''<div class="g-ref"><h2>Gross-to-net reference</h2>
<p>Hourly gross is rate times hours per week times weeks per year. Overtime hours in this tool are paid at 1.5 times the straight rate and added before tax. A yearly salary is divided by 52 weekly, 26 biweekly, 24 semimonthly, or 12 monthly pays. Income tax uses the same 2026 federal and state tables as the <a href="../tax-calculator/">income tax calculator</a>. Payroll tax still applies to 401(k) deferrals. Health premiums, HSAs, local tax, and state disability insurance are not removed.</p>
<div class="g-ex"><h3>Worked example: $25 an hour, 40 hours, 52 weeks, single, Texas</h3>
<p>Gross is $52,000. Federal tax after the $16,100 standard deduction is $4,060. Social Security is $3,224. Medicare is $754. Annual take-home is $43,962. Each biweekly check is about $1,690.85. Each semimonthly check is about $1,831.75. Five overtime hours a week add 5 times $37.50 times 52, or $9,750, before you rerun the tax.</p></div>
<h3>Pay-frequency traps</h3>
<p>Two biweekly checks are not a month. Multiply by 26 and divide by 12 for a monthly budget figure, about $3,663.51 in the example above. A year with 27 biweekly pay dates is a calendar accident this model does not invent; it always uses the count you select. Semimonthly is the 15th and the last day in real payroll and is simply one twenty-fourth of annual net here.</p>
<h3>Checking a stub</h3>
<p>Social Security year-to-date divided by 0.062 should be close to FICA wages if you are under the $184,500 wage base and have no pre-tax plan that is exempt from FICA. Federal withholding on a stub moves with the W-4. Use this page to compare states or a raise, then confirm the first live check. Pair the monthly net with the <a href="../budget-calculator/">budget calculator</a>.</p></div>''')

_r('sales-tax', '''<div class="g-ref"><h2>Sales-tax arithmetic for 2026</h2>
<p>Added tax is the taxable base times the rate. Tax included in the price comes back out by dividing by 1 plus the rate. The state rate in the menu is exact. The local add-on is the Tax Foundation population-weighted average as of July 1, 2026, not the rate at one street address. Type My exact rate when you are charging a customer. The published source is the <a href="https://taxfoundation.org/data/all/state/2026-sales-tax-rates-midyear/">Tax Foundation 2026 midyear sales-tax update</a>.</p>
<div class="g-ex"><h3>Worked examples at 8.25 percent</h3>
<p>On $100.00, tax is $8.25 and the customer pays $108.25. Working backwards, $108.25 divided by 1.0825 returns $100.00. On $249.99 tax-inclusive, the pre-tax price is $249.99 divided by 1.0825, or $230.94, and the tax is $19.05 after cent rounding. Three lines of $24.00, $10.50, and $6.00 are a $40.50 base and $3.34 of tax if you round the tax to the cent. An unticked Taxable line stays in the amount due and out of the base.</p></div>
<h3>States with no statewide tax</h3>
<p>Alaska, Delaware, Montana, New Hampshire, and Oregon have a zero statewide rate in this table. Alaska still has local tax, averaging about 1.82 percent. A zero state rate is not a promise that your city is zero. Food, clothing, freight, and services are often exempt or taxed at another rate even when the general rate is right.</p>
<h3>Bookkeeping</h3>
<p>Record the pre-tax amount as sales and the tax as a liability until you remit it. Discount the subtotal before you compute tax, which is what the <a href="../receipt-quote-maker/">receipt and quote maker</a> does. The compare-all-states table shows the same basket at each combined average. It is not a nexus opinion. Use the <a href="../invoice/">invoice generator</a> when you need the tax as a single rate on a document.</p></div>''')

_r('loan-calculator', '''<div class="g-ref"><h2>Amortization formula</h2>
<p>The fixed payment is P times r divided by 1 minus (1 plus r) to the power of minus n. P is the amount borrowed, r is the annual rate divided by 12, and n is the number of months. Interest for a month is the opening balance times r. Principal is the payment minus that interest. An extra payment reduces principal after interest. The schedule stops when the balance is gone. Fees, taxes, insurance, and prepayment penalties are not added.</p>
<div class="g-ex"><h3>Worked example: $50,000 at 9 percent for 5 years</h3>
<p>r is 0.0075 and n is 60. The payment is $1,037.92. Total interest is about $12,275. The first month's interest is $375.00, so about $662.92 is principal. An extra $100 a month finishes in 54 payments and saves about $1,387 of interest. A mortgage-shaped example, $320,000 at 6.5 percent for 30 years, is about $2,022.62 a month of principal and interest and about $408,142 of interest over the full term, before tax and insurance.</p></div>
<h3>How to read the schedule in the books</h3>
<p>Split each cash payment into interest expense and a reduction of the loan liability. The yearly summary matches a 12-month close. The monthly table is the subledger. Presets are example fillers, not credit offers. Type the note rate if you want cash interest, and label the export if you typed an APR that already folds in fees. For a one-line payment without a schedule, the loan box on the <a href="../biz-calc/">business calculators</a> page uses the same formula.</p></div>''')

_r('profit-margin', '''<div class="g-ref"><h2>Margin, markup, price, and break-even</h2>
<p>Margin is profit divided by price. Markup is profit divided by cost. Price for a target margin is cost divided by 1 minus the margin. Price for a target markup is cost times 1 plus the markup. Break-even units are fixed costs divided by price minus variable cost, rounded up. A simple return on the unit cost is profit divided by cost, which is the markup, not the margin.</p>
<div class="g-ex"><h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>Cost $40 and price $65: profit $25, margin 38.46 percent, markup 62.5 percent. A 40 percent margin on that cost needs 40 divided by 0.60, or $66.67. A 40 percent markup needs only $56.00 and yields a 28.6 percent margin. Fixed costs of $5,000, price $25, variable cost $10: contribution is $15, so break-even is 334 units and $8,350 of revenue. A further $3,000 profit goal needs 534 units.</p></div>
<h3>Where the margin goes</h3>
<p>Discounts and card fees are often missing from the unit cost people type. A 15 percent discount and a 3 percent fee can turn a 38 percent calculator margin into something near 20 percent in the accounts. Put inbound freight in cost if you cannot sell without it. Gross margin in the ledger is net sales minus cost of goods sold, divided by net sales. The price ladder shows common target margins on the cost you typed. The <a href="../biz-calc/">business calculators</a> page is the same margin math without the ladder. The <a href="../freelance-rate/">freelance rate calculator</a> is the service-business version, where the cost is your year rather than a unit.</p></div>''')

_r('freelance-rate', '''<div class="g-ref"><h2>The rate formula</h2>
<p>Gross the income you want to keep by dividing by 1 minus your tax reserve. Add yearly business expenses. Multiply by 1 plus the profit buffer. Divide by billable hours, which are working weeks times hours per week times the billable share. Day rate is hourly times a full day. A project price is hourly times the hours you will actually spend, including revisions.</p>
<div class="g-ex"><h3>Worked example using the defaults</h3>
<p>$60,000 take-home at a 25 percent reserve is $80,000 before tax. Plus $10,000 of costs is $90,000. A 10 percent buffer makes $99,000 of revenue to bill. 48 weeks times 40 hours times 70 percent is 1,344 billable hours. The floor is $73.66 an hour, about $589 a day, and about $1,473 for a 20-hour project before you round up. Quoting $75 or $80 leaves room for scope you did not write down.</p></div>
<h3>Tax reserve versus a W-2 calculator</h3>
<p>The percent is a planning reserve. On $80,000 of self-employment profit, self-employment tax alone is about 15.3 percent of 92.35 percent, or about $11,304, before income tax and before the deduction for half of that tax. The <a href="../tax-calculator/">wage calculator</a> does not compute that. Owner draws are not an expense. Typing 100 percent billable underprices the week you spend on proposals. Use the <a href="../invoice/">invoice generator</a> to bill the rate you settled, and the <a href="../budget-calculator/">budget calculator</a> to park the tax reserve as a need.</p></div>''')

_r('budget-calculator', '''<div class="g-ref"><h2>A monthly plan that matches cash</h2>
<p>Budget take-home pay, not gross wages. The 50/30/20 checkpoint puts about half of take-home toward needs (housing, utilities, groceries, insurance, basic transport, minimum debt payments), about 30 percent toward wants, and about 20 percent toward savings and extra debt payments. The tool converts weekly or yearly amounts onto the same footing and shows each category as a percent of income.</p>
<div class="g-ex"><h3>Worked example: $4,000 take-home</h3>
<p>Needs about $2,000, wants about $1,200, savings about $800. If a biweekly net is $1,690.85, the monthly average is that amount times 26 divided by 12, or $3,663.51. Budget the average, not two checks, or two months a year will look richer than they are. Rent of $1,800 already consumes most of the needs band. The rule does not say to skip rent. It says the rest of the plan has to be honest about what is left.</p></div>
<h3>Irregular months and business cash</h3>
<p>Freelance income belongs in the plan at a conservative month, with surplus moved to a tax reserve. That reserve is a need, not a want. Annual insurance should be entered as a yearly figure so one month is not a fake crisis. A shortfall is money you do not have. Covering it from savings is a transfer, not new income. Get the take-home figure from a stub or from the <a href="../paycheck-calculator/">paycheck calculator</a>. Keep business margin on the <a href="../profit-margin/">profit margin calculator</a> so a good sales month is not mistaken for personal spending money.</p></div>''')

_r('invoice', '''<div class="g-ref"><h2>What the document has to say</h2>
<p>A useful invoice names you, names the client, carries a unique number and a date, lists quantity and price, shows tax if you charge it, states the total, and says how to pay. The heading should say Invoice before you are paid and Receipt after. This page applies one tax rate to the subtotal. It does not send email, reconcile a bank, or file a return. Download PDF from the print dialog by choosing Save as PDF. There is no watermark and no cap.</p>
<div class="g-ex"><h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>A $1,000 subtotal at 8.25 percent is $82.50 of tax and a $1,082.50 total. If you also need a discount or a mix of exempt lines, build the figures on the <a href="../sales-tax/">sales tax calculator</a> or the <a href="../receipt-quote-maker/">quote maker</a>, then invoice the agreed total. Do not hide tax inside the line and also add a tax percent, or you will tax the tax. Number the next invoice INV-002, not another INV-001.</p></div>
<h3>Filing the PDF</h3>
<p>Keep a file name with the number and the client. Export the lines to Excel or CSV for the sheet you actually keep. Cash-basis businesses record income when they are paid. Accrual businesses record it when they earn it. A deposit is a liability until the work is done, not the whole invoice. The archive is your folder. The page does not keep a copy.</p></div>''')

_r('receipt-quote-maker', '''<div class="g-ref"><h2>Receipt, estimate, and quote</h2>
<p>A receipt proves payment and should name the method. An estimate is allowed to move when the job becomes clearer. A quote is a fixed offer with a valid-until date. Tax is computed after the discount, which is the order most US sales-tax rules expect. A deposit is an amount due to start, not a second taxable sale. The balance is the total minus the deposit.</p>
<div class="g-ex"><h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>A $500 subtotal with a 10 percent discount is $450. Tax at 8 percent is $36. The total is $486. A $150 deposit leaves a $336 balance to invoice later. Do not tax the deposit again on the final invoice. If the valid-until date passes and your supplier price moved, send a new quote rather than honoring a dead number by accident.</p></div>
<h3>From quote to books</h3>
<p>A quote is not revenue. A deposit you have not earned is a liability if you keep accrual records. When the client accepts, create the invoice on the <a href="../invoice/">invoice generator</a> with the same lines. Check the rate on the <a href="../sales-tax/">sales tax calculator</a> before you type the percent, especially if one line is exempt. The PDF is what you send. The spreadsheet export is what you file. Logos stay in the <a href="../logo-maker/">logo maker</a> so this document prints without a missing image.</p></div>''')

_r('biz-calc', '''<div class="g-ref"><h2>Four calculations, one page</h2>
<p>Margin is profit over price. Markup is profit over cost. Added sales tax is amount times rate. Tax-inclusive price divided by 1 plus the rate returns the pre-tax amount. The loan payment uses the standard monthly amortization formula. Break-even units are monthly fixed costs divided by price minus variable cost per unit. A simple ROI on a campaign is gain minus cost, divided by cost.</p>
<div class="g-ex"><h3>Defaults you can check by hand</h3>
<p>Cost 40 and price 65: profit 25, margin 38.46 percent, markup 62.5 percent. Amount 100 at 8.875 percent: tax 8.88 and total 108.88. A $25,000 loan at 7.5 percent for 5 years: payment $500.95, interest about $5,056.92. Fixed costs $3,000 a month, price $25, variable cost $10: 200 units and $5,000 of revenue. A $2,000 ad buy that yields $2,600 of gross profit is a 30 percent ROI. Dividing by revenue instead of cost would flatter a weak spend.</p></div>
<h3>When to open the dedicated tool</h3>
<p>Use the <a href="../profit-margin/">profit margin calculator</a> for a target price, a ladder, and a profit goal. Use the <a href="../sales-tax/">sales tax calculator</a> for 2026 state rates and mixed baskets. Use the <a href="../loan-calculator/">loan calculator</a> for a schedule and extra principal. Fixed costs on this page are per month. A yearly rent figure typed here without dividing by 12 overstates the units you must sell.</p></div>''')

_r('meeting-cost', '''<div class="g-ref"><h2>What a meeting costs</h2>
<p>Multiply people by the average hourly rate by the length in hours. A salary becomes an hourly rate by dividing by 2,080, which is 40 times 52. The timer uses that same rate so the on-screen number is cost so far. The yearly figure multiplies a weekly meeting by about 52. Benefits are not added unless you type a loaded rate.</p>
<div class="g-ex"><h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>Six people at $45 for one hour is $270. Thirty minutes is $135. Loaded at 30 percent, the rate is $58.50 and the hour is $351. Weekly, the unloaded meeting is about $14,040 a year. A $90,000 salary is $43.27 an hour before any burden. Eight people at $60 for a 15-minute standup is $120 a day. None of those figures include the cost of the decision being wrong, which is the point of putting the number next to the agenda.</p></div>
<h3>Using it without fooling yourself</h3>
<p>Count only people who attend. Use the internal cost, not a client's billable rate, unless the client is paying for the hour. A freelancer's relevant rate is the one from the <a href="../freelance-rate/">freelance rate calculator</a>, not take-home divided by 2,080. The timer is not a timesheet. Export the assumptions if the number is going into a planning note, and refresh the length if the laptop slept through part of the meeting.</p></div>''')

_r('pdf-tools', '''<div class="g-ref"><h2>Merge, split, and images to PDF</h2>
<p>Merge writes a new PDF in the order you select. Split keeps the pages you list, such as 1-3, 5, counting PDF pages from 1 even when the footer says otherwise. Save each page emits one file per page. Images to PDF puts each picture on its own page. The work uses pdf-lib in the browser. There is no upload, no account, and no OCR. Password-protected files need to be unlocked first or the result can be blank.</p>
<div class="g-ex"><h3>A receipt packet that will actually email</h3>
<p>Ten phone photos dropped in raw can be tens of megabytes. Compress each image to about 1600 pixels and about 75 percent JPEG in the <a href="../image-compress/">image compressor</a>, then build the PDF here. A packet of already-small JPEGs is the difference between an attachment and a bounce. For print, 200 to 300 pixels per inch at the printed size is enough for ordinary pages. A full letter-size photo at 300 DPI is about 2550 by 3300 pixels. You do not need the 48-megapixel original inside the PDF.</p></div>
<h3>Failure modes</h3>
<p>A truncated download, a renamed non-PDF, or an encrypted file fails honestly. Memory, not a hidden page cap, limits very large jobs. Merge in batches if a long photo PDF stalls, and close other tabs. The result is an ordinary PDF, not PDF/A and not a fresh digital signature. Keep the originals. The page has no trash can on a server because the bytes never left the device.</p></div>''')

_r('image-compress', '''<div class="g-ref"><h2>Width, quality, and format</h2>
<p>Maximum width scales the image down and keeps the aspect ratio. It does not upscale. Quality defaults to 75 percent for JPEG and WEBP. PNG stays lossless, so photographs barely shrink. JPEG fills transparency with white. Redrawing on a canvas also drops most EXIF, including GPS, which is useful for a public upload and a reason to keep the camera original.</p>
<div class="g-ex"><h3>Targets that work in practice</h3>
<p>Website photos: 1600 pixels on the long side, JPEG or WEBP at 75 percent, often 150 to 400 KB from a camera original. Instagram posts: about 1080 pixels. Email: about 1200 pixels so a few images stay under a typical attachment budget. Thumbnails: about 400 pixels. A product shot that must land under 200 KB usually needs a slightly lower quality or a 1200-pixel width, especially if the scene is busy. Flat color compresses further than foliage. Judge the preview, not the slider.</p></div>
<h3>What not to compress</h3>
<p>A logo you will print should stay SVG or a PNG at print size. A 75 percent JPEG of a wordmark grows fuzzy edges. Screenshots with small text belong in PNG or a high WEBP setting. Animated GIFs are flattened to a single bitmap. If the new file is larger, the original was already more compressed than your setting. Pair this page with the <a href="../image-convert/">converter</a> when you need a different format, and with <a href="../pdf-tools/">PDF tools</a> when the photos are going into a document.</p></div>''')

_r('image-convert', '''<div class="g-ref"><h2>PNG, JPG, and WEBP</h2>
<p>JPG is the interchange format for marketplaces, email, and older tools. PNG keeps transparency and sharp edges and is the right file for a logo or a screenshot. WEBP is usually smaller than JPEG at the same quality and is what you want on a modern site. This page does not resize. Quality from 0.1 to 1 applies to JPG and WEBP. 0.9 is a light touch. PNG ignores it. JPG paints transparency with the background color you choose.</p>
<div class="g-ex"><h3>A catalog batch</h3>
<p>Convert once from the master, not from a JPEG you already saved three times. Keep the SKU in the file name. Leave the transparent PNG of the logo alone and convert only the product photographs. If the destination is a web page at 1600 pixels, compress after the conversion. A 6000-pixel JPG is still a 6000-pixel JPG. HEIC from an iPhone opens only when the browser can decode it. Otherwise export JPEG from Photos first. Safari is the browser that usually can. A renamed PDF will fail decode, because the extension is not trusted.</p></div>
<h3>Color and privacy</h3>
<p>Drawing to a canvas can shift a wide-gamut photo into standard color. Check one frame at 100 percent before you convert a season of shots. Nothing is uploaded. A closed tab drops the batch, so download as you go. The <a href="../image-compress/">compressor</a> is the next step when the pixels are right but the file is heavy.</p></div>''')

_r('audio', '''<div class="g-ref"><h2>Trim, fade, WAV, and 128 kbps MP3</h2>
<p>The selection is a start time and an end time on the file you open. Fades are gain ramps on those samples. WAV export is 16-bit PCM at the source sample rate, mono or stereo as decoded. MP3 export is a fixed 128 kbps file from the in-browser LAME encoder. There is no 192 or 320 option on this page. The page does not fetch audio from a URL.</p>
<div class="g-ex"><h3>Ringtone and voice targets</h3>
<p>A phone ringtone wants about 20 to 30 seconds. iPhone ringtones are limited to about 30 seconds, so set the end at or before 30. A 1 to 2 second fade-out stops a click. Voice is fine at 128 kbps. A minute of stereo WAV at 44.1 kHz is about 10 MB. The same minute at 128 kbps is about 1 MB. Preview the selection. The shortest cut is a tenth of a second. Files over about 200 MB are rejected so the tab does not die, and decode fails when the browser does not know the codec.</p></div>
<h3>Rights and the next edit</h3>
<p>Trim files you own or may use. Re-encoding MP3 to MP3 makes music duller. Trim from WAV when you can, and keep the WAV if you will edit again. For a video source and a higher MP3 bitrate, use <a href="../video-to-mp3/">video to MP3</a>, which offers 128, 192, and 320. This page only trims and fades. It does not denoise or normalize.</p></div>''')

_r('video-to-mp3', '''<div class="g-ref"><h2>Extracting audio you already have</h2>
<p>Open a video file on the device. The browser decodes it and the page writes MP3 or WAV. MP3 choices are 128 kbps for speech, 192 kbps as the default balance, and 320 kbps for the largest MP3 this tool makes. WAV is lossless and large. A five-minute stereo program is about 7 MB at 192 kbps and on the order of 55 MB as 48 kHz WAV. MOV and HEVC files fail when the browser cannot decode them. Export an H.264 MP4 with AAC from the app that made the video and try again.</p>
<div class="g-ex"><h3>What this is not</h3>
<p>It is not a link downloader. A YouTube address, a stream, or any URL is out of scope. Use a screen recording or a project file you have rights to. Long videos can run out of memory because the audio is decoded locally. That limit is the privacy model: the file is not sent to a server that could finish the job. Close other tabs or shorten the source. There is no stem split and no volume repair. Trim notification sounds afterward in the <a href="../audio/">audio trimmer</a>.</p></div>
<h3>Before you publish</h3>
<p>Listen to the head and tail. Keep the video until the export is right. A meeting recording is still confidential after it becomes an MP3. WAV is the archive. MP3 is the copy you send. 128 kbps is enough for a spoken email attachment. Use 192 or 320 when the program includes music you care about hearing accurately.</p></div>''')

_r('word-counter', '''<div class="g-ref"><h2>Counts that match the way editors talk</h2>
<p>Words split on whitespace. Characters are reported with spaces and without. Reading time uses about 238 words per minute. Speaking time uses about 150. A 600-word description is about two and a half minutes to read and about four minutes to speak. The keyword list skips common function words. HTML pasted from a template counts the tags, so paste the visible text if you want a reading length.</p>
<div class="g-ex"><h3>Lengths businesses actually hit</h3>
<p>Meta descriptions are often cut near 150 to 160 characters. Many ad headlines sit under 30 characters. A short email that gets finished is often under 200 words. A teaching article commonly runs 800 to 1,500 words, or about four to seven minutes at 238 words per minute. Resume bullets are healthier at one or two lines, roughly 15 to 30 words. Paste a bullet from the <a href="../resume/">resume tool</a> if you cannot tell whether it has swollen. None of these are scores. They are limits you would rather see here than in a rejected upload.</p></div>
<h3>What the keyword list will not do</h3>
<p>Frequency is not a ranking. A product name at 8 percent of the words may be stuffing. A name that never appears may be a vague page. Languages that do not put spaces between words will not match an English word count. The character count still means something. Exports save the counts locally. The text itself never needs an account, and it is not uploaded.</p></div>''')

_r('password', '''<div class="g-ref"><h2>Entropy, length, and where the secret should live</h2>
<p>Characters come from crypto.getRandomValues, not from a guessable clock. A 16-character password from about 95 printable symbols carries on the order of 100 bits, about 6.6 bits per character. Cut the length to 8 and you cut that space in half in bit terms. Turn symbols off and each character is worth less, so add length until the strength line is still strong. The line estimates the settings. It does not search breach lists.</p>
<div class="g-ex"><h3>A usable policy for a small team</h3>
<p>16 characters or more, a unique password for every account, stored in a password manager. The email account and the manager's own master password should be longer. If a site rejects symbols, keep digits on and lengthen the password rather than dropping to 8 characters. This page will not export or send the password to a phone, because a notes file is a copy you will lose track of. Copy it into the manager and the account, then leave the page. Reloading throws the secret away on purpose.</p></div>
<h3>What the generator will not pretend</h3>
<p>It does not make a memorable sentence. It does not know a particular site's rules beyond the sets you enable. It does not upload the result, and there is no account to recover it from. A perfectly random 6-character password is still short. Prefer this page or the manager's generator over a pet's name and a year.</p></div>''')

_r('business-name', '''<div class="g-ref"><h2>Ideas are not clearances</h2>
<p>Each style draws from its own prefix and suffix list and mixes in the keyword you type. Another click draws again. The list is not a search of existing companies and not a legal opinion. Modern, classic, playful, and tech change the tone of the filler words. They do not check a registry.</p>
<div class="g-ex"><h3>A practical shortlist</h3>
<p>Generate several batches. Keep five names you can still spell an hour later. Say each one on a phone call. Look at it in the <a href="../logo-maker/">logo maker</a> at favicon size. A name that only works in a long script will fail as an icon. Then search the trademark database for your class of goods, your state or national business registry, the domain, and the social handles. Budget the hour before you order packaging. A name that is free as an LLC can still infringe a federal mark.</p></div>
<h3>Tone and risk</h3>
<p>A playful suffix can undersell a firm that needs to look established. A generic word plus Co is hard to find in search. Do not pick a name that implies a license you do not hold. Export the shortlist and mark which checks you finished. The file is a worksheet you made locally, not a registration. International sales need a check in each country where you will actually sell.</p></div>''')

_r('email-signature', '''<div class="g-ref"><h2>A signature that survives email clients</h2>
<p>Paste into the client's signature settings, not into a single message. Gmail: Settings, See all settings, General, Signature. Outlook on the web: Settings, Mail, Compose and reply. Apple Mail: Settings, Signatures. Paste as rich text. Send yourself a test and open it on a phone. The block is simple HTML with inline styles and one accent color, because email clients strip ordinary stylesheets.</p>
<div class="g-ex"><h3>What to include</h3>
<p>Name, role, company, one phone, one email, one website. Skip the award list and the novel-length disclaimer. Take the accent hex from the <a href="../color-palette/">color palette</a> so the signature, the logo, and the site match. Do not embed a huge photo. This generator does not host images. A photo that depends on a link will break, or it will be blocked, and the reply will look suspicious. Full https links survive better than bare www text.</p></div>
<h3>Shared brands</h3>
<p>Agree the line order and the color once. Each person changes only name, title, and phone. Inconsistent signatures look forwarded. Dark mode may recolor type. The PDF export is a record of the details, not the thing you install. The copy button is the install. Nothing is uploaded, and there is no tracking pixel in the block.</p></div>''')

_r('color-palette', '''<div class="g-ref"><h2>Harmonies you can actually ship</h2>
<p>Analogous hues sit together and feel calm. Complementary hues sit opposite and give you an accent, at the cost of more tension. Triadic hues are three evenly spaced colors and work when one of them dominates. Start from the logo color. Random is a way out of a rut, not a brand system. Copy hex or CSS variables so the site, the signature, and the logo use the same values.</p>
<div class="g-ex"><h3>Contrast and a small palette</h3>
<p>Body text wants at least a 4.5 to 1 contrast ratio against its background. Large text wants at least 3 to 1. A bright accent can be a button and still fail as small type. Use near-black or near-white for paragraphs. One background, one text color, one accent, and one optional support color is enough for a small business. Five loud colors make a menu look unfinished. The swatch label flips between dark and light so you can read the hex. That is not a full audit of your CSS.</p></div>
<h3>Print and dark mode</h3>
<p>Screen hex is not CMYK ink. Ask for a press proof before you print packaging. Very light tints vanish on cheap paper. Dark mode needs a second pair, not the same hex with the brightness down. Export the brand sheet and file it next to the SVG from the <a href="../logo-maker/">logo maker</a>. Colors as such are not owned, but a distinctive combination can still be part of someone's trade dress. This page does not clear that, and it does not upload the palette.</p></div>''')

_r('qr-bio', '''<div class="g-ref"><h2>A code that stores the destination</h2>
<p>The PNG encodes the URL or the text you typed. There is no redirect and no expiry on our side. The code dies only when the destination dies or the print is unreadable. A shortener you do not control can still expire. For anything you will print and forget, encode the final https address. Error correction handles modest damage, not a torn code. bio.html is a single file you host yourself. It does not phone home.</p>
<div class="g-ex"><h3>Print sizes</h3>
<p>On a card or a table tent, print at least about 2 centimeters (0.8 inches) square, with a quiet white margin. Posters need to be readable from the distance people stand. Black on white scans. Low-contrast brand colors and glossy lamination often do not. Print a proof at 100 percent scale and scan it with a phone in the real lighting. Fit-to-page blurs the modules. A long URL makes the modules smaller. Shorten the address or print larger.</p></div>
<h3>What to encode</h3>
<p>A menu PDF or your own page, a booking link, or public contact text. Not a private invoice, not a password, not a Wi-Fi secret you would not paint on the window. Anyone who can see the code can open it. If you want one printed code whose destination can change, point it at a file you host and edit that file. If you want the code itself to be permanent, point it at a URL you will not rename. The <a href="../barcode-label/">barcode maker</a> is the tool for UPC and Code 128. This page is for QR and a simple link page.</p></div>''')

_r('policy', '''<div class="g-ref"><h2>Drafts, not a law firm</h2>
<p>The generator fills a plain-English privacy, terms, or refund draft with the business name, site, email, location, and the topics you tick. It is a starting point for a small site. It is not legal advice, not a GDPR record, not a PCI certificate, and not a HIPAA notice. Have counsel read it if you take payments, run ads, serve children, handle health data, or sell into the EU or UK. Delete any sentence that describes a business you do not run.</p>
<div class="g-ex"><h3>What a true privacy section contains</h3>
<p>What you collect, why, who else sees it, how long you keep it, and how a person emails you. If a processor charges the card, say that you do not store the full number if that is true. If you show ads or analytics, say so. A 30-day refund you will not honor is worse than a shorter window you will. Custom work, digital goods, and food often need an exception written in the open. Match the refund text to what your payment platform requires and to what the counter will actually do.</p></div>
<h3>Publishing</h3>
<p>Put the text on a page you control and date it. Update it before you add a pixel or a newsletter, not after a complaint. This site's own practices are described on the <a href="../privacy/">GWB privacy policy</a> and <a href="../terms/">terms</a>. Those pages are about GWB, not a template for your shop. Export a snapshot for your files. The generator does not host the policy and will not notice when your business changes. The draft is assembled locally.</p></div>''')

_r('resume', '''<div class="g-ref"><h2>Duties into bullets</h2>
<p>Lead with a verb, name the work, and stop on a result a stranger can picture. Volume, money, time, or a percent all count. If you cannot measure the result, state an honest scope: a team of four, 30 accounts, a class of 25. Three to five bullets for a recent role is enough. Put the line that matches the job you want first. This page rewrites one duty at a time. It does not lay out the page and it does not invent employers.</p>
<div class="g-ex"><h3>Before and after</h3>
<p>Handled customer emails is a duty. Answered about 40 customer emails a day and cut the typical reply from a day to under four hours is a bullet, if both numbers are true. About 200 tickets a week is fair when you can show the queue. A 40 percent improvement you cannot explain is not. Look at a CRM export or a calendar before you guess. A bullet that needs three clauses is usually two bullets. The <a href="../word-counter/">word counter</a> will show when a line has passed about 30 words.</p></div>
<h3>What to leave out</h3>
<p>Pronouns, slogans, and references available upon request. Do not put a client's confidential metric, a password, or a government ID in a file you will upload to a job site. Keep the resume as text so an applicant-tracking system can read it. The verb styles only change the opening. Edit the rest until the sentence is something you will say out loud in an interview. The rewrite stays in the browser.</p></div>''')

_r('logo-maker', '''<div class="g-ref"><h2>A mark, a master file, and a favicon pack</h2>
<p>The preview is your words plus one of the simple icons drawn for this tool, a layout, a system font, and colors. You can use that result commercially. It is not a trademark search and not a registration. SVG is the master: it stays sharp for the web and for a printer who takes vectors. PNG is the bitmap marketplaces ask for, with transparency. JPEG is usually the wrong logo file.</p>
<div class="g-ex"><h3>Favicon sizes</h3>
<p>The pack includes favicon.ico at 16, 32, and 48, PNGs at 16, 32, 180, 192, and 512, a web manifest, and a head snippet. Tabs use the small sizes. Phones use the 180-pixel touch icon when the site is saved to a home screen. 512 serves an installable icon. A logo that only lives on the about page does not become the tab icon until those files are on the site. Test the mark at 16 pixels. If it turns into a stripe, use the icon alone in the tab and the full name in the header.</p></div>
<h3>Fonts, color, and print</h3>
<p>System fonts can substitute on another computer. The PNG and the favicon bake the pixels, so hand those to a printer or an upload form. Take the hex from the <a href="../color-palette/">color palette</a> and reuse it in the <a href="../email-signature/">email signature</a>. A storefront banner needs the SVG, not a 512-pixel PNG stretched across a wall. Clear the business name as a trademark before you print a thousand bags. Nothing you type is uploaded.</p></div>''')

_r('barcode-label', '''<div class="g-ref"><h2>Retail barcodes versus your own labels</h2>
<p>UPC-A is 12 digits for US and Canadian retail. EAN-13 is 13 digits for international retail. The last digit is a check digit this page can compute. Both belong on a product only when the number is licensed to you, typically through GS1. Code 128 is for internal SKUs, bins, and packing lists. It can hold letters and does not require GS1, and a grocery lane will not treat it as a UPC. QR on this page is for a URL or a note. A point-of-sale laser may ignore it. Use the <a href="../qr-bio/">QR maker</a> when the code is only a link.</p>
<div class="g-ex"><h3>Sheets and a line of text</h3>
<p>One code per line, optionally code, name, and price separated by vertical bars. The name and price are printed. They are not hidden inside a UPC, which holds only the digits. Avery 5160 is 30 labels of 2.625 by 1 inch. Avery 5163 is 10 labels of 4 by 2 inches and is easier to scan. Avery 5167 is 80 tiny labels and only suits a short code. Print the PDF at 100 percent on US Letter. Fit to page walks the bars off the stickers. Proof on plain paper and hold it over a label sheet.</p></div>
<h3>Scanners</h3>
<p>Failure is usually a missing quiet zone, bars that were scaled, glare, or a color other than black on white. Test with the counter scanner, not only a phone. Do not invent a UPC from random digits and ship it to a retailer. Use Code 128 with your own prefix, such as SKU-10042, for anything that is only yours. The drawing stays in the browser. The number on a product becomes public when you stick it on the product.</p></div>''')
